Implementation: Step 1: Sign in to the AWS Management Console 
1. Go to the AWS Management Console. 
2. Sign in with your AWS account credentials. 
Step 2: Navigate to AWS Lambda
1. In the AWS Management Console, find and select Lambda from the Services menu. 
Step 3: Create a New Lambda Function
1. Click on Create function. 
2. You will be presented with four options:
o Author from scratch: 
Create a function from scratch.
o Use a blueprint: Start with a sample Lambda function. 
o Container image: Deploy a function defined as a container image. 
o Browse serverless app repository: Use a pre-built serverless application. 
For simplicity, select Author from scratch. 
Step 4: Configure the Function
1. Function name: Enter a unique name for your Lambda function (e.g., MyFirstLambda). 
2. Runtime: Choose the runtime environment for your code (e.g., Python 3.x, Node.js 14.x, etc.). 
3. Role: o If you dont have an existing IAM role, select Create a new role with basic Lambda permissions. 
o If you have an existing IAM role, you can use that by selecting Use an existing role. 
Step 5: Write the Lambda Function Code 
1. Once the function is created, you will be redirected to the functions configuration page. 
2. Scroll down to the Code section. 
3. In the code editor, you can write your Lambda function code. For example, if you are using Python: def lambda_handler(event, context): return { ‘statusCode’;: 200, ‘body’;: ‘Hello, AWS Lambda!’ } 
4. The lambda_handler function is the entry point for your Lambda function. The event parameter contains the input data, and the context parameter provides runtime information. 
Step 6: Test the Function 
1. At the top of the function configuration page, click on Test. 
2. Create a new test event by choosing Create new test event. 
3. Provide a name for the test event (e.g., TestEvent), and you can use the default event template.
4. Click Create and then click Test again to invoke the function. 
Step 7: Review the Execution Results 
1. After testing, you can review the execution results in the Execution result section. 
2. If everything is set up correctly, you should see the output {“statusCode&quot”: 200, “body”: “Hello, AWS Lambda!”}. 
Step 8: Deploy the Function (Optional) 
1. If you want to deploy the Lambda function as part of a more complex architecture, consider using AWS services like API Gateway, S3, or Step Functions to trigger the Lambda function. 
Step 9: Monitor and Manage 
1. AWS Lambda provides built-in monitoring through Amazon CloudWatch. You can view logs and performance metrics to manage and troubleshoot your Lambda function. 
